Rodney,

Thanks for the read-through. I took both suggestions and made them real:

1. SEIR/EXPO now actually drives output. Previously the SEIR submodel
   only ever ran once (on the first record) and its result was
   discarded (that commented-out POSQ=F*POSQ line in sun.f). Now, in
   the new mode, SEIR state evolves every record instead of resetting,
   and EXPO's result actually scales POSQ.

2. Replaced the 1/IREC decay with something tied to elapsed calendar
   days instead of raw record count. Turns out rgo_data.prn itself has
   a 24-day gap in it (day 2 jumps straight to day 26) that the old
   record-count decay was completely blind to. The new decay sees it.

I kept this as an opt-in "robust" dynamics mode rather than replacing
the old behavior outright -- the original FORTRAN-matching path is
still the default and is still tested byte-for-byte against SUN.CSV,
so nothing that depended on exact reproduction of the old code broke.
Run it with --dynamics robust (or SunSimulation(..., dynamics="robust")
in code) to get the new behavior.

Added a full test suite for the new path, including a test that
specifically exercises that 24-day gap scenario. Re-rendered
analysis_report.pdf/html with an updated Recommendations section
marking these two items done.

Didn't touch the other two items you might notice in there -- running
against the full historical RGO dataset, and validating against the
Bogdan et al. figures -- since I don't have either of those on hand.
Send them over if you've got them and I'll fold them in.

Tagged this as v2.0.0, pushed to main.

